  "_reject_python_only_comment": [
    "A deliberate, tested asymmetry. JavaScript has one number type, so it cannot see the",
    "difference between 2 and 2.0 -- by the time JSON.parse is done, the float spelling is",
    "gone and the value is a safe integer. Python keeps the distinction and refuses the float.",
    "",
    "This asymmetry is safe in the only direction that matters. A producer that emits 2.0",
    "makes Python refuse the payload outright, so the mission or decision is never created.",
    "It can never make Python emit 2.0 where JavaScript emits 2, which is the silent hash",
    "fork this whole file exists to rule out. Fail-closed on one side beats agreement on a",
    "wrong byte, so the rule stands and is pinned here rather than quietly deleted.",
    "",
    "js_canonical records exactly what JavaScript produces, so a future change to the",
    "JavaScript encoder cannot widen the asymmetry without this test noticing."
  ],
  "reject_python_only": [
    { "name": "float_integral_value", "value": 2.0, "js_canonical": "2" },
    { "name": "negative_float_zero", "value": -0.0, "js_canonical": "0" },
    { "name": "exponent_integral", "value": 1e2, "js_canonical": "100" },
    { "name": "nested_float_integral", "value": { "a": 2.0 }, "js_canonical": "{\"a\":2}" },
    { "name": "float_integral_in_array", "value": [1, 2.0, 3], "js_canonical": "[1,2,3]" }
  ]
